{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>If there arise among you a prophet.</b> Who says about himself that he is a prophet, and that the Holy One, blessed be He, spoke to him while awake or in a dream. <b>And he gives you a sign,</b> in heaven, in the manner of what is written about Gideon (Judges 6): ״ועשית לי אות שאתה מדבר עמי״. <b>Or a wonder,</b> on earth — even so, you shall not listen to him. Thus did Rashi explain. And if you say, why did the Holy One, blessed be He, give him dominion to perform a sign — for HaShem your Elohim tests you." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, explained 'a sign or a wonder': a sign is an indication concerning a thing that will happen afterward in its likeness, in the manner of what is written (Numbers 2:2) ״איש על דגלו באותות״ — for when the prophet says that a certain thing is going to happen in a likeness that will follow it, this is called a sign, as it is said (Isaiah 7:14): ״לכן יתן ה' הוא לכם אות״; and the word is derived from אתה. And 'a wonder' refers to something newly made before us with a change in the nature of the world, in the manner of what is said (II Chronicles 32:31): ״לדרוש המופת אשר היה בארץ״, (Joel 3:3): ״ונתתי מופתים בשמים ובארץ״, and so forth; and it is an abbreviated word: מופת — מופלאת (a wondrous sign). And in Egypt there were signs and wonders, as it is written (Deuteronomy 4:34): ״במסות באותות ובמופתים״ — 'signs' are what Moses would announce to them before they came to be; 'wonders' are what were done at their time in the way of marvel and change of nature, in the manner of the staff: it was called a sign when he announced it beforehand, and it was called a wonder when it became a serpent before Pharaoh and before his servants. End quote." "<b>And it is</b> well known that a person is not held to be a prophet until he gives a sign and the sign comes about according to his words, and it is required that all his words be fulfilled without anything lacking — for if even a single dot is missing, he is not a prophet. And once he has been established as a prophet among people by virtue of the signs he gave them concerning the future and all of them were fulfilled, if he prophesies concerning an evil and it does not come, they do not regard him as a false prophet; for a prophet is not tested by his prophecy as to whether he is true or not except in the matter of the good about which he prophesied, whether for an individual or for the many, and not one word of the good that he spoke may fall. And so Jeremiah said to Hananiah son of Azzur the prophet (Jeremiah 28:9): ״אשר ינבא לשלום בבא דבר הנביא יודע הנביא אשר שלחו ה' באמת״. But concerning an evil matter, if his words were not fulfilled, they do not suspect him, for perhaps they repented, and the Holy One, blessed be He, relents and repents concerning the evil, but He does not relent and repent concerning the good. And I call upon faithful witnesses for myself: Jonah and Isaiah. For Jonah son of Amittai, when he prophesied and said (Jonah 3:4): ״עוד ארבעים יום ונינוה נהפכת״ — because they repented, the Holy One, blessed be He, relented concerning the evil and had compassion on them. And so too Isaiah, when he said to Hezekiah (II Kings 20): ״מות תמות״ — and there is no doubt that a decree of death had been decreed upon him from before the Omnipresent — because he repented, He extended his life by fifteen years. And so the words of the prophets that were from the Holy One, blessed be He, were nullified concerning the evil, because He, may He be exalted, created repentance before He created the world, and it overrides everything."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:10.152101138Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}